{
 "about": "The codec's integers and the canonical bytes' numbers, the same on both sides (docs/dev/handwriting.md 'Channels' and 'The bytes of an upload'): gamma/ink.py encode_points and frontend/src/ink/ink.js encodePoints turn each `encode` case's `samples` (channels `ch`) into exactly `pts`, and gamma/ink.py dumps and ink.js serializeInk write each `files` case's `file` (JSON text, its literals as written) as exactly `bytes`, so a file the server writes (an import, a merge, the upgrade step) is byte for byte the one a client writes for the same strokes. Every value is rounded as Math.round rounds it: to the nearest integer, a half going up (0.5 is 1, -0.5 is 0, 2.5 is 3), never to the even one. x and y are times 100; p is held to 0..1, then times 1000; t is as it is; a is held to 0..90; z is as it is; w is held to 0..4, then times 1000; o is held to 0..1, then times 1000; a letter not known here is the sample's next `rest` value. A channel a sample lacks or holds as null is p 0.5, t 0, a 90, z 0, w 1, o 1, rest 0. x, y, t, w and o are written as deltas after the first sample. A number is written as JSON.stringify writes it (ECMAScript Number::toString): the shortest digits that read back as the same double, plainly from 1e-6 up to 1e21 and as d[.ddd]e+n or d[.ddd]e-n outside (1e-7, 1.5e+21), minus zero as 0, a whole float as an integer, an integer past 2^53 as the double a client reads. Each `encode` case is `{note, ch, samples, pts}`, each `files` case `{note, file, bytes}`; backend/tests/test_shared_fixtures.py also checks that dumps writes the parsed file (parse_ink) as the same bytes.",
